.TH BOOTCONFIG 8 2026-09-21 "Linux 7.0.9" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
bootconfig \- apply, delete or show the boot configuration attached to an initrd image
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bootconfig
.RB [ \-l ]
.I file
.br
.B bootconfig \-a
.I config
.I initrd
.br
.B bootconfig \-d
.I initrd
.br
.B bootconfig \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bootconfig
manages the boot configuration ("bootconfig") that the Linux kernel reads
from the end of an initial RAM disk (initrd) image at boot.
A boot configuration is a text file of structured key-value pairs; the
keys under
.B kernel
and
.B init
are added to the kernel command line and to the arguments of the init
process.
The syntax is described in
.I \%Documentation/admin\-guide/bootconfig.rst
in the Linux source.
.PP
An attached boot configuration is stored at the end of the image as the
configuration text followed by a NUL byte, NUL padding that aligns the
total file size to a multiple of 4 bytes, and a 20-byte footer.
The footer holds the size of the data including the padding and a
checksum of the text and its NUL byte, each as a 32-bit little-endian
integer, followed by
the 12-byte magic string
.RB \(dq #BOOTCONFIG\en \(dq.
.PP
Without an option,
.B bootconfig
shows the boot configuration in
.IR file .
If
.I file
ends with a bootconfig footer, the attached configuration is checked
against the size and checksum in the footer, parsed, and shown.
Otherwise, if
.I file
is no larger than 32767 bytes, it is parsed as a boot configuration
source file and shown; a syntax error is reported with its line and
column.
A larger file that has no footer produces no output.
.PP
By default the configuration is shown in a compact tree form: keys that
share a single path are joined with dots, a key with several subkeys
opens a brace block, values are quoted (with single quotes if the value
contains a double quote), array elements are separated by commas, and
each entry ends with a semicolon.
With
.BR \-l ,
each key is shown on its own line in the form
.IB key " = " value
with the full dotted key; a key without a value is shown with the value
.BR \(dq\(dq .
.SH OPTIONS
Only one of
.BR \-a ,
.B \-d
and
.B \-l
may be given.
.TP
.BI \-a " config"
Parse the boot configuration source file
.I config
and, if it is valid, attach it to
.IR initrd ,
replacing any boot configuration already attached.
.B bootconfig
prints the two file names, the number of parse nodes, the size of the
data in bytes (including the terminating NUL byte, excluding padding) and
the checksum.
If
.I config
cannot be parsed, the error is reported with its line and column and
.I initrd
is not modified.
If the new data cannot be written completely,
.B bootconfig
truncates
.I initrd
to the size it had before the new data was appended.
.TP
.B \-d
Remove the boot configuration attached to
.I initrd
by truncating the file.
If no boot configuration is attached,
.I initrd
is left unchanged and the command succeeds.
.TP
.B \-l
Show the configuration of
.I file
as a list of
.IB key " = " value
lines instead of the compact tree form.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a usage message and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B bootconfig
exits with status 0 on success.
On failure it exits with the low eight bits of a negative error number
(for example 254 for a file that does not exist), or with 255 after
printing the usage message, which includes
.BR \-h .
When a boot configuration source file shown without
.B \-a
or
.B \-d
fails to parse, the exit status is instead the low eight bits of the
size of the file, which may be 0.
.SH NOTES
.B bootconfig
is built with the kernel's own boot configuration parser, so a
configuration that it accepts is accepted by the kernel.
The parser limits a configuration to 32767 bytes of data (for
.BR \-a ,
including the terminating NUL byte that
.B bootconfig
adds) and to 8192 nodes.
.PP
The kernel must be built with
.BR \%CONFIG_BOOT_CONFIG .
It reads the configuration attached to the initrd only if
.B bootconfig
is given on the kernel command line or the kernel was built with
.BR \%CONFIG_BOOT_CONFIG_FORCE .
The boot loader must pass the exact size of the initrd image; otherwise
the kernel cannot find the footer.
The configuration in use by the running kernel can be read from
.IR \%/proc/bootconfig .
.SH EXAMPLES
Attach
.I my.bconf
to an initrd image, replacing any existing configuration:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
bootconfig \-a my.bconf /boot/initrd.img
.EE
.in
.PP
Show the attached configuration as a list, then remove it:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
bootconfig \-l /boot/initrd.img
bootconfig \-d /boot/initrd.img
.EE
.in
